1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,100
A swinging modern masterpiece.

2
00:00:04,800 --> 00:00:06,220
At home in the deep woods.

3
00:00:07,200 --> 00:00:08,940
Taking four years to complete,

4
00:00:09,780 --> 00:00:13,260
it became the man-made link that connects the divided state of Michigan.

5
00:00:14,040 --> 00:00:16,940
Now, the Mackinac Bridge on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,260
Upon completion in 1957, it was the longest single-span suspension bridge on Earth.

7
00:00:36,740 --> 00:00:42,720
But unlike its record-breaking predecessors, it doesn't support the chaotic hustle and bustle of New York City.

8
00:00:43,360 --> 00:00:47,440
Nor does it stand at the gateway to California's golden promised land.

9
00:00:49,340 --> 00:00:54,220
The Mackinac Bridge could be the engineering and architectural jewel of any major metropolis.

10
00:00:54,221 --> 00:01:01,220
Instead, it connects two small Michigan towns, Mackinac City and St. Ignace.

11
00:01:01,221 --> 00:01:05,220
From an engineering standpoint, the Mackinac Bridge is unique.

12
00:01:05,221 --> 00:01:09,220
But when I look at the bridge, it's simply a magnificent structure.

13
00:01:09,221 --> 00:01:13,220
The bridge is graceful to look at and be around.

14
00:01:13,221 --> 00:01:16,220
It is the icon of Michigan.

15
00:01:16,221 --> 00:01:20,220
It's interesting that it's so far away from any major metropolitan area.

16
00:01:20,221 --> 00:01:24,220
It's 400 miles from Chicago and nearly 300 miles from Detroit.

17
00:01:26,220 --> 00:01:34,220
Separated by lakes Michigan and Huron, the state of Michigan is really two peninsulas that come closest together in the five-mile-wide Mackinac Straits.

18
00:01:36,220 --> 00:01:44,220
The word Mackinac is derived from the Native American name for the region, Michela Mackinac, which many translate as land of the great turtle.

19
00:01:44,221 --> 00:01:50,720
The peculiar spelling of Mackinac, which ends in an AC, is often attributed to early French

20
00:01:50,721 --> 00:01:51,720
settlers.

21
00:01:51,721 --> 00:01:57,560
The bridge is the only physical connection between Michigan's pastoral northern mainland

22
00:01:57,561 --> 00:02:05,220
and heavily forested Upper Peninsula.

23
00:02:05,221 --> 00:02:09,580
It stands strong against some of the harshest weather and natural conditions found anywhere

24
00:02:09,581 --> 00:02:13,380
in the United States.

25
00:02:13,381 --> 00:02:18,480
stormy days, extreme amount of winds, a lot of environmental conditions in the Straits of

26
00:02:18,481 --> 00:02:21,520
Maconah, both high temperature changes in the wintertime.

27
00:02:21,521 --> 00:02:23,280
We record temperatures well below

28
00:02:23,281 --> 00:02:29,184
zero, and in the summertime, temperatures can rise as much as 100 degrees.

29
00:02:29,185 --> 00:02:30,860
At nearly five miles,

30
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:35,054
the structure is really many bridges combined to make one.

31
00:02:35,055 --> 00:02:37,360
Roughly 30 small backspan bridges

32
00:02:37,361 --> 00:02:41,460
attached to the main and most visually stunning part of the span, the suspension bridge.

33
00:02:43,380 --> 00:02:53,840
Strung between a pair of colossal anchorages and over two 552-foot towers, from end to end, it stretches an amazing 8,614 feet.

34
00:02:57,080 --> 00:03:05,840
Suspension bridges are unique in both design and construction, while other spans rely on simple supports or the uplifting forces exerted by cantilevers.

35
00:03:05,841 --> 00:03:06,821
cantilevers.

36
00:03:06,822 --> 00:03:13,200
Suspension bridges hang from two main cables strung over towers and secured at

37
00:03:13,201 --> 00:03:14,621
anchorage points.

38
00:03:14,622 --> 00:03:21,560
The design allows the bridge to be both incredibly strong and deceivingly flexible.

39
00:03:23,020 --> 00:03:27,080
The Mackinac Bridge is a very fluid, very moving bridge.

40
00:03:27,081 --> 00:03:29,980
It is designed to be that way. It is not

41
00:03:29,981 --> 00:03:31,460
fixed, it is not solid.

42
00:03:31,461 --> 00:03:36,220
In a high wind, the bridge will rotate to the east or to the west up to 35

43
00:03:36,221 --> 00:03:41,549
feet. It will move up and down 10 feet by temperature alone.

44
00:03:41,550 --> 00:03:43,680
The towers will actually

45
00:03:43,681 --> 00:03:47,082
move in toward each other 10 to 15 feet.

46
00:03:47,083 --> 00:03:52,100
Though unnoticed by most motorists, the key to the bridge's

47
00:03:52,101 --> 00:03:54,207
flexibility lies on the roadway.

48
00:03:54,208 --> 00:03:58,620
Here we are on the roadway surface of the bridge near the expansion

49
00:03:58,621 --> 00:04:00,260
Joint Assembly at the South Tower.

50
00:04:00,700 --> 00:04:03,900
Designed to expand and contract with changes in temperature,

51
00:04:04,320 --> 00:04:06,080
changes in wind pressure,

52
00:04:06,360 --> 00:04:09,900
and loading of vehicles that go across the structure, mainly trucks.

53
00:04:10,740 --> 00:04:12,640
As the bridge expands and contracts,

54
00:04:13,040 --> 00:04:16,000
the remainder of the structure also rises and falls.

55
00:04:17,680 --> 00:04:20,920
But there's one feature of the bridge no motorist can miss.

56
00:04:21,540 --> 00:04:25,000
The two center lanes are built of open steel grates.

57
00:04:25,001 --> 00:04:29,480
It is an open surface that you can see right through.

58
00:04:29,980 --> 00:04:34,460
If anyone were afraid of heights, they might have problems walking around on the open grating

59
00:04:34,461 --> 00:04:38,760
because it's about 150 feet down to the water surface.

60
00:04:41,360 --> 00:04:46,700
This isn't meant to torture acrophobes, but is actually an ingenious design feature of the bridge.

61
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:51,240
Winds off the Great Lakes have been clocked at over 100 miles per hour.

62
00:04:51,241 --> 00:04:58,240
Instead of catching it like a sail, the aerodynamic road surface allows the wind to harmlessly flow through.

63
00:04:58,241 --> 00:05:06,240
The combination of open roadway, low side railing and slender suspension cables gives the structure a very airy feel.

64
00:05:06,241 --> 00:05:10,240
For some, crossing the bridge can feel like flying.

65
00:05:10,241 --> 00:05:13,240
For others, it's a bit overwhelming.

66
00:05:15,240 --> 00:05:18,240
The bridge authority runs a driver's assistance program.

67
00:05:18,241 --> 00:05:18,941
program.

68
00:05:18,942 --> 00:05:23,600
Once or twice a day, bridge workers take the wheel from frightened motorists and

69
00:05:23,601 --> 00:05:25,304
taxi them across.

70
00:05:25,305 --> 00:05:32,120
But for almost everyone else, the bridge has become a cherished part

71
00:05:32,121 --> 00:05:38,040
of the landscape. The economic prosperity gained by connecting the local communities

72
00:05:38,041 --> 00:05:42,640
and the simple convenience the bridge offers has forever changed the state of Michigan.

73
00:05:42,641 --> 00:05:48,640
just as the first bridge backers envisioned it would in the late 1800s.

74
00:05:49,140 --> 00:05:52,720
In the 1880s, when the Brooklyn Bridge opened up in New York,

75
00:05:53,260 --> 00:05:56,460
and a grocer, of all people, around the Straits area,

76
00:05:57,020 --> 00:06:01,360
got the idea of publishing a picture of the Brooklyn Bridge on his grocery bags,

77
00:06:01,660 --> 00:06:04,700
and he superimposed it with the state of Michigan.

78
00:06:05,400 --> 00:06:09,580
And a lot of people started talking about the possibility of building a bridge at that point in time.

79
00:06:09,581 --> 00:06:15,200
But that talk didn't become serious until the introduction of the automobile

80
00:06:15,201 --> 00:06:19,000
and subsequent road construction increased access into the Upper Peninsula.

81
00:06:20,780 --> 00:06:24,920
In 1923, the state began running ferries across the Straits of Mackinac.

82
00:06:26,000 --> 00:06:28,980
But harsh weather hindered the boat's passage most of the year.

83
00:06:30,320 --> 00:06:34,240
The only other option for motorists who wanted to get from the Lower to the Upper Peninsula

84
00:06:34,241 --> 00:06:38,820
was an over-30-hour four-state odyssey around the shores of Lake Michigan.

85
00:06:39,580 --> 00:06:48,080
By the 1930s, local businessmen realized a bridge was necessary if the area was to grow

86
00:06:48,081 --> 00:06:49,080
financially.

87
00:06:49,081 --> 00:06:54,040
For years, the bridge had been a political football, kicked around between the two major

88
00:06:54,041 --> 00:06:58,680
parties as to who would get the credit for building it.

89
00:06:58,681 --> 00:07:00,568
But the governor, Governor G.

90
00:07:00,569 --> 00:07:04,800
Menon Williams, to his everlasting credit, appointed an authority

91
00:07:04,801 --> 00:07:08,760
of six men who were above politics.

92
00:07:08,761 --> 00:07:11,838
The chairman, everybody agreed, would be Prentice M.

93
00:07:11,839 --> 00:07:14,560
Brown, who had been the United States Senator,

94
00:07:15,380 --> 00:07:19,520
and at that time was chairman of the Detroit Edison Company.

95
00:07:21,560 --> 00:07:24,660
Everyone involved realized finding money would be difficult.

96
00:07:26,840 --> 00:07:32,080
Some engineers claimed that given the crushing winter ice and gale force winds of spring and fall,

97
00:07:32,600 --> 00:07:35,120
no bridge could be built to withstand the harsh conditions.

98
00:07:35,121 --> 00:07:41,840
even if it could be built it most definitely would experience construction delays during

99
00:07:41,841 --> 00:07:48,800
the winter months because of the frozen straits and the potential source of bridge revenue traffic

100
00:07:48,801 --> 00:07:54,020
was erratic at best during holidays or at the beginning or end of hunting season

101
00:07:54,021 --> 00:08:00,200
ferry passengers could wait 19 hours but most of the year traffic was extremely light

102
00:08:01,060 --> 00:08:04,360
The head of the bridge authority understood the problems all too well.

103
00:08:06,060 --> 00:08:13,320
My dad, Prentice Brown, had good connections with the markets in Michigan and New York,

104
00:08:13,440 --> 00:08:17,860
and he knew getting $100 million, which was the estimated cost, would be a problem.

105
00:08:18,400 --> 00:08:21,920
The legislature said, no way is the state going to back this thing.

106
00:08:22,640 --> 00:08:29,240
But they may pass legislation to allow the authority to go out and raise these funds

107
00:08:29,241 --> 00:08:32,060
without the full faith and credit of Michigan.

108
00:08:34,020 --> 00:08:37,680
In 1950, the state of Michigan authorized the Bridge Authority

109
00:08:37,681 --> 00:08:40,360
to try to sell their idea to the American public.

110
00:08:41,560 --> 00:08:43,740
Hat in hand and with almost no backing,

111
00:08:44,280 --> 00:08:47,460
the first thing they needed was a plan, or a set of plans.

112
00:08:50,460 --> 00:08:53,120
Fortunately for them, a bridge-designing superstar

113
00:08:53,121 --> 00:08:55,520
had been eyeing the Straits for nearly 20 years.

114
00:08:55,521 --> 00:08:59,391
Having designed over 400 projects, Dr. David B.

115
00:08:59,392 --> 00:09:03,180
Steinman was eager to make the bridge his own.

116
00:09:04,700 --> 00:09:07,580
He was an engineer who owned his own firm in New York.

117
00:09:08,360 --> 00:09:13,440
At just over five feet tall, he was larger than life and exactly what the bridge authority needed.

118
00:09:13,860 --> 00:09:17,340
As a boy, he had been brought up in the shadow of the Brooklyn Bridge.

119
00:09:17,920 --> 00:09:23,020
I knew early on that he was going to dedicate his life to bridge designing.

120
00:09:23,021 --> 00:09:24,580
At least he told me that.

121
00:09:24,581 --> 00:09:28,260
He was small, but a booming voice.

122
00:09:28,720 --> 00:09:34,380
If he had any failing, it was a lack of potesty, shall we say.

123
00:09:34,720 --> 00:09:39,140
Dr. Steinman was actually one of the engineers that came into the area in the 1930s

124
00:09:39,141 --> 00:09:42,720
to investigate the possibility of building a bridge across the Straits of Mackinac.

125
00:09:43,140 --> 00:09:45,340
One thing that people don't realize about bridge engineers,

126
00:09:45,900 --> 00:09:48,200
a lot of times they don't wait to be contacted.

127
00:09:48,340 --> 00:09:51,440
They look on their maps and see where bridges need to be built.

128
00:09:52,300 --> 00:09:55,140
The Mackinac Bridge is the crowning achievement of his career,

129
00:09:55,720 --> 00:09:59,320
and he built this bridge to withstand extreme amount of winds.

130
00:10:00,020 --> 00:10:04,400
At the time the bridge was built, the largest or the highest recorded wind speed,

131
00:10:04,520 --> 00:10:07,020
I believe, was around 78 miles an hour on the Straits,

132
00:10:07,600 --> 00:10:11,700
and he built the bridge to withstand winds exceeding 600 miles an hour.

133
00:10:13,780 --> 00:10:15,600
Overcompensation? Perhaps.

134
00:10:15,880 --> 00:10:19,800
But only 10 years earlier, the people of Washington saw firsthand

135
00:10:19,801 --> 00:10:21,980
and how much damage an ill wind can cause.

136
00:10:24,120 --> 00:10:26,600
The engineers of the Tacoma Narrows Bridge

137
00:10:26,601 --> 00:10:27,560
had pushed the envelope,

138
00:10:28,140 --> 00:10:31,000
a disastrous combination of too light and too thin

139
00:10:31,001 --> 00:10:32,700
over such a long span.

140
00:10:34,600 --> 00:10:37,240
Steinman knew his creation was going to break records,

141
00:10:37,460 --> 00:10:39,320
but he didn't want the dubious honor

142
00:10:39,321 --> 00:10:41,660
of designing the longest span ever to collapse.

143
00:10:42,900 --> 00:10:44,820
He vowed the Mackinac Bridge would withstand

144
00:10:44,821 --> 00:10:46,100
not only wind and current,

145
00:10:46,600 --> 00:10:47,920
but the test of time as well.

146
00:10:49,800 --> 00:10:56,600
His vision included a robust foundation of mammoth concrete piers, supporting trusses

147
00:10:56,601 --> 00:11:03,660
to stiffen and stabilize the span, and special aerodynamic grading on the roadway, all meant

148
00:11:03,661 --> 00:11:07,480
to keep the bridge standing strong.

149
00:11:07,481 --> 00:11:13,760
When questioned by a newspaper man as to what would happen if a Great Lakes oar boat struck

150
00:11:13,761 --> 00:11:18,680
the bridge's main tower support, Steinman quipped, the boat will sink with a serious

151
00:11:18,681 --> 00:11:19,677
loss of life.

152
00:11:19,678 --> 00:11:25,120
Steinman believed so strongly in the project, he committed a portion of

153
00:11:25,121 --> 00:11:27,750
his staff to draw up blueprints on spec.

154
00:11:27,751 --> 00:11:30,840
The move put him on top of a very short list of

155
00:11:30,841 --> 00:11:32,440
potential bridge designers.

156
00:11:32,441 --> 00:11:38,800
Dr. Steinman deserves a lot of credit for the success of the Mackinac Bridge because

157
00:11:38,801 --> 00:11:45,140
of his willingness to go out on a limb to draw up the plans for bidding purposes without

158
00:11:45,141 --> 00:11:46,729
getting any fees for that.

159
00:11:46,730 --> 00:11:50,580
And until we actually sold the bonds, he stood to lose a lot of

160
00:11:50,581 --> 00:11:54,411
money, probably a quarter of a million dollars or more.

161
00:11:54,412 --> 00:11:56,640
So he told me, and I believe him

162
00:11:56,641 --> 00:11:59,920
because engineers in New York City don't come cheap.

163
00:12:02,540 --> 00:12:07,400
Even with Steinman's elaborate plans, none of the large financial bond companies usually

164
00:12:07,401 --> 00:12:11,940
associated with huge public works programs were willing to take the risk.

165
00:12:11,941 --> 00:12:13,060
But one relatively

166
00:12:13,061 --> 00:12:18,100
small underwriter, the Allen Company in New York, saw a chance for profit.

167
00:12:21,180 --> 00:12:28,080
And on February 17, 1954, they cut a certified check guaranteed by private bonds for over

168
00:12:28,081 --> 00:12:34,760
$96 million. It was barely enough money to cover the proposed construction schedule,

169
00:12:35,220 --> 00:12:40,410
which meant the bridge needed to be finished in collecting tolls within four years.

170
00:12:40,411 --> 00:12:41,160
The race was

171
00:12:41,161 --> 00:12:41,861
on.

172
00:12:41,862 --> 00:12:50,160
The highest wind speed ever recorded on the Mackinac Bridge was 117 miles per hour.

173
00:12:50,161 --> 00:12:55,195
The Mackinac Bridge will return on Modern Marvels.

174
00:12:55,196 --> 00:12:58,820
We now return to the Mackinac Bridge

175
00:12:58,821 --> 00:13:07,054
on Modern Marvels. From its creation in 1837, Michigan had been a state divided.

176
00:13:07,055 --> 00:13:07,980
With both

177
00:13:07,981 --> 00:13:13,580
its heavily forested peninsulas separated by two of the largest lakes on earth.

178
00:13:13,581 --> 00:13:21,320
But in 1954, an army of engineers, craftsmen and builders descended on the narrow straits

179
00:13:21,321 --> 00:13:24,860
of Mackinac to link the state with concrete and steel.

180
00:13:24,861 --> 00:13:29,740
The actual construction of the Mackinac Bridge began in 1954 and they assembled the largest

181
00:13:29,741 --> 00:13:33,820
group of maritime construction equipment for a peacetime project that had ever been assembled

182
00:13:33,821 --> 00:13:35,540
in the world.

183
00:13:35,541 --> 00:13:39,740
For engineers, choosing the bridge site was easy.

184
00:13:39,741 --> 00:13:41,940
It was the shortest distance between two points.

185
00:13:41,941 --> 00:13:45,020
The hard part was getting it built.

186
00:13:45,021 --> 00:13:49,460
To stand up to the forces of nature, the bridge needed a firm footing.

187
00:13:49,461 --> 00:13:54,720
The design called for the construction of 33 concrete foundations, or piers, built in

188
00:13:54,721 --> 00:13:55,720
the water.

189
00:13:55,721 --> 00:14:01,200
Pier 17, one of two water anchorages for the bridge's suspension cables, was the starting

190
00:14:01,201 --> 00:14:03,200
point.

191
00:14:03,201 --> 00:14:10,200
From observation towers, engineers triangulated the pier's proper location.

192
00:14:10,201 --> 00:14:22,200
About a mile from shore, barge workers lowered the first of three enormous 135 by 35 by 75 foot steel frames into 88 feet of water.

193
00:14:22,201 --> 00:14:32,200
Because everything depended on a secure anchorage, workers used spud piles to nail the steel cage into the mud and silt of the lake bed, known as overburden.

194
00:14:32,201 --> 00:14:40,200
Like giant metal tent stakes, the piles were pounded by crane operators through slots in the frame, down to solid bedrooms.

195
00:14:42,200 --> 00:14:49,200
Once the frame was secured, two equally monstrous steel supports were lowered and fastened together by divers.

196
00:14:50,200 --> 00:14:57,200
Around this connected framework, bridgemen pounded interlocking metal plates to create the submerged outer shell of the anchor pier.

197
00:14:57,201 --> 00:15:03,200
anchor pier. We're here at the south anchor pier of the Mackinac Bridge down

198
00:15:03,201 --> 00:15:07,280
near the water level. This anchor pier was constructed using the cofferdam

199
00:15:07,281 --> 00:15:11,600
method of construction which is a large open type structure built of sheet

200
00:15:11,601 --> 00:15:15,020
piling. You can see here the sheet piling that is left in place with the

201
00:15:15,021 --> 00:15:21,680
interlocking devices. With the interlocking metal plates driven to bedrock

202
00:15:21,681 --> 00:15:25,800
and completely surrounding the inner frames, the cofferdam was complete.

203
00:15:27,300 --> 00:15:30,320
Crane operators then removed the lake bed from inside the dam.

204
00:15:31,540 --> 00:15:36,320
With nothing but exposed bedrock remaining, workers began pumping in concrete.

205
00:15:36,680 --> 00:15:38,040
A lot of concrete.

206
00:15:38,680 --> 00:15:41,980
Enough to form over 10 miles of a four-lane highway.

207
00:15:43,720 --> 00:15:47,020
When you look at the Mackinac Bridge, 75% of it is underwater.

208
00:15:47,021 --> 00:15:51,160
You don't realize how big that structure really is if you can see the whole thing.

209
00:15:51,680 --> 00:15:58,680
Once completed, the bridge's substructure would weigh in at more than 900,000 tons,

210
00:15:58,681 --> 00:16:02,680
dwarfing the over 100,000 ton superstructure.

211
00:16:02,681 --> 00:16:09,680
In spring of 1956, the underwater foundations were being finished.

212
00:16:09,681 --> 00:16:17,680
Pier 17's huge footing was in place, and workers started the equally enormous job of pouring the over 10-story top structure.

213
00:16:17,681 --> 00:16:23,820
Deep inside, builders encased the retaining hooks or eye bars, but would hold fast one

214
00:16:23,821 --> 00:16:26,280
end of the bridge's suspension cables.

215
00:16:26,281 --> 00:16:33,920
The finished product was a 180,000-ton man-made mountain, used to both support and anchor the

216
00:16:33,921 --> 00:16:38,560
bridge.

217
00:16:38,561 --> 00:16:43,740
Another early challenge for designers, receding Ice Age glaciers had dug an underwater canyon

218
00:16:43,741 --> 00:16:45,280
in the middle of the straits.

219
00:16:45,281 --> 00:16:52,220
At the center, the bottom lay beneath 200 feet of water and nearly 400 feet of loose sand and mud.

220
00:16:52,880 --> 00:16:56,480
Though the suspension bridge would hang gracefully above the deepest part of the canyon,

221
00:16:57,020 --> 00:17:02,460
on either side the two 552-foot towers had to be firmly planted on bedrock.

222
00:17:05,720 --> 00:17:08,780
To get there, engineers designed a cutting caisson.

223
00:17:08,781 --> 00:17:15,800
built from a number of massive metal rings the caisson was created by stacking the pieces one

224
00:17:15,801 --> 00:17:21,560
atop the other once connected it formed an open shaft to the bottom of the lake

225
00:17:23,700 --> 00:17:29,480
it was 116 feet diameter with the first section being tapered kind of giving it a cutting edge

226
00:17:29,481 --> 00:17:35,460
to cut its way into the overburden and at the same time they would excavate material out of

227
00:17:35,461 --> 00:17:40,080
center of the caisson and it was essentially drove itself down to bedrock

228
00:17:40,081 --> 00:17:48,780
assembled on land workers dubbed the four-story behemoths Paul Bunyan's Donuts despite the

229
00:17:48,781 --> 00:17:54,180
playful name getting them in place was hard work they had trouble because of the winds and the

230
00:17:54,181 --> 00:17:58,860
current flowing through the Straits area where these large caissons were would spin like tops

231
00:17:58,861 --> 00:18:04,900
For passing spectators, the site was nearly comical.

232
00:18:04,901 --> 00:18:08,880
The workers, however, were being taught a tough lesson in physics.

233
00:18:08,881 --> 00:18:13,940
Once 300 tons begins to move, it's very difficult to stop.

234
00:18:13,941 --> 00:18:18,440
And they used these large cables connected to ships out there, these big freighters,

235
00:18:18,441 --> 00:18:20,100
in order to prevent them from spinning.

236
00:18:20,101 --> 00:18:21,440
But they had trouble.

237
00:18:21,441 --> 00:18:25,040
One day they might spin one way clockwise, and another day they might spin counterclockwise.

238
00:18:25,041 --> 00:18:27,040
They really didn't know.

239
00:18:27,041 --> 00:18:33,860
Despite their wayward behavior above the water, once submerged, the performance of the caissons was nearly perfect.

240
00:18:34,500 --> 00:18:39,140
As barge workers sank the massive steel structure, they added additional pieces to the top.

241
00:18:40,380 --> 00:18:45,980
Simultaneously, crane operators using clamshell steam shovels dug the overburden from inside.

242
00:18:46,980 --> 00:18:54,960
The increased weight and diminishing interior muck caused the caissons' tapered edge to gouge its way down to bedrock and a firm foundation.

243
00:18:57,040 --> 00:19:04,040
By May 6, 1955, both caissons were in place.

244
00:19:04,041 --> 00:19:08,040
The next step involved filling the massive hollow tubes.

245
00:19:08,041 --> 00:19:16,040
Over the course of the project, workers pumped over 160,000 cubic yards of concrete into the two tower supports.

246
00:19:16,041 --> 00:19:25,040
103,000 cubic yards were poured in just 31 days to set a new world record for underwater concrete pouring.

247
00:19:25,041 --> 00:19:29,580
With the foundation laid, on July 2, 1955, the U.S.

248
00:19:29,581 --> 00:19:33,140
Steel Corporation took on the formidable

249
00:19:33,141 --> 00:19:36,800
task of raising the superstructure.

250
00:19:36,801 --> 00:19:43,540
With its arrival, a small army of workers descended on the unsuspecting local communities.

251
00:19:43,541 --> 00:19:48,800
During the course of the project, over 2,500 men worked the bridge site.

252
00:19:48,801 --> 00:19:53,640
For the tiny town of Mackinac City, it was like the circus had come to town and decided

253
00:19:53,641 --> 00:19:54,640
to stay.

254
00:19:54,641 --> 00:19:59,260
When all these construction people, especially the iron workers, converged on the area,

255
00:19:59,460 --> 00:20:01,780
I don't think the town really knew what was going to happen.

256
00:20:02,640 --> 00:20:05,640
I kind of related to an old west town when they discovered gold.

257
00:20:05,940 --> 00:20:10,120
Just all these people coming into the area, and they were different.

258
00:20:11,500 --> 00:20:14,500
They had a different type of lifestyle than most of the regulars did.

259
00:20:14,660 --> 00:20:16,980
It was a change for the locals, put it that way.

260
00:20:17,460 --> 00:20:21,880
There were a lot of businesses that kind of came with them and left with them.

261
00:20:21,881 --> 00:20:26,880
Some of those businesses catered to the iron workers' every need and passion.

262
00:20:26,881 --> 00:20:34,880
There's one place in town called Lil's Fatio, and they get a plate of ribs and a bath and a bull for three dollars,

263
00:20:34,881 --> 00:20:38,880
and that made their night, and they might get two or three of them.

264
00:20:41,880 --> 00:20:47,880
Today, some of the men who worked on the Mighty Mac periodically get together and remember their time raising the bridge.

265
00:20:47,881 --> 00:20:52,880
In a restaurant below the Mackinac Bridge Museum, they've assembled for drinks.

266
00:20:52,881 --> 00:20:57,880
The ironworkers have the best record for getting in, doing the job and getting out.

267
00:20:57,881 --> 00:21:01,880
The ironworkers are trained to be safe and ironworkers love to be safe.

268
00:21:01,881 --> 00:21:06,880
And as an old saying goes, you can count the joes by the fingers and the toes.

269
00:21:06,881 --> 00:21:09,880
And I think if you look around here, you'll see everybody has got all of them.

270
00:21:09,881 --> 00:21:11,880
Some of them is getting a little wrinkled.

271
00:21:11,881 --> 00:21:12,880
No, I'm not.

272
00:21:12,881 --> 00:21:13,880
Just kidding, isn't it?

273
00:21:13,881 --> 00:21:14,880
My son is.

274
00:21:14,881 --> 00:21:16,880
Sounds good, sounds good.

275
00:21:16,881 --> 00:21:28,040
I was in the Riveting Gang, so we were coming up behind them.

276
00:21:28,440 --> 00:21:30,860
We had a scaffold that went all the way around the outside,

277
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,740
and we had to pull that damn thing up with hand crabs.

278
00:21:34,700 --> 00:21:35,680
Boy, that thing was heavy.

279
00:21:35,681 --> 00:21:41,200
The superstructure of the Mackinac Bridge is very similar to a child director set.

280
00:21:41,380 --> 00:21:44,740
The towers were constructed in a steel yard in Pennsylvania,

281
00:21:44,741 --> 00:21:49,160
and they actually assembled these 552 feet tall towers

282
00:21:49,161 --> 00:21:50,920
horizontally on the ground at Pennsylvania

283
00:21:50,921 --> 00:21:52,260
to make sure all the parts fit right.

284
00:21:52,800 --> 00:21:54,000
And then they took it all apart

285
00:21:54,001 --> 00:21:56,140
and brought it in by rail into St. Ignis.

286
00:21:56,620 --> 00:21:59,840
And from St. Ignis, they took everything out on barges

287
00:21:59,841 --> 00:22:02,800
and on ships where they hoisted all the pieces together.

288
00:22:05,760 --> 00:22:08,540
When the height became too great to reach from the water,

289
00:22:08,840 --> 00:22:10,900
workers constructed a special creeper derrick.

290
00:22:10,901 --> 00:22:15,160
Like a spider, the creeper Derek climbed the towers.

291
00:22:16,860 --> 00:22:23,380
Once in position, it lifted 80-ton sections of steel to gangs of men who secured the pieces level by level.

292
00:22:25,700 --> 00:22:28,000
This was the first ironworker job I had.

293
00:22:28,440 --> 00:22:34,160
We started out about 50 feet in the air, and as we went up, we went up to 552 feet.

294
00:22:34,800 --> 00:22:37,040
And it was quite a change from being on the ground.

295
00:22:37,320 --> 00:22:39,900
And you were so nervous at times.

296
00:22:39,901 --> 00:22:49,240
and you never really got used to it, but after a while you, I don't know how to say it,

297
00:22:49,540 --> 00:22:51,360
you was okay up there, I guess.

298
00:22:53,680 --> 00:22:59,880
Over the next five months, bridge men toiled to lift and secure the 13,000 tons of steel

299
00:22:59,881 --> 00:23:01,180
that made up the two towers.

300
00:23:02,060 --> 00:23:07,280
On October 22, 1955, the top piece of the first tower was set in place.

301
00:23:08,040 --> 00:23:11,460
the bridge workers high above the straits decided on a fitting celebration.

302
00:23:13,000 --> 00:23:16,780
They stood up there, 550 feet above the water,

303
00:23:18,180 --> 00:23:22,420
waving a flag in a 35-mile-an-hour southwest wind.

304
00:23:23,680 --> 00:23:26,120
And the flag was just whipping back and forth.

305
00:23:26,820 --> 00:23:30,260
And there they were, waving with their hard hats on.

306
00:23:30,720 --> 00:23:32,660
And it was amazing.

307
00:23:32,661 --> 00:23:44,100
On that day, the tower and underwater caisson comprised a structure 753 feet tall, ten full

308
00:23:44,101 --> 00:23:49,700
stories higher than the Washington Monument.

309
00:23:49,701 --> 00:23:55,000
One milestone was beneath their work belts, but the celebration was short-lived.

310
00:23:55,001 --> 00:24:02,060
The bridge men knew they had another two full years of toil and bad weather ahead.

311
00:24:02,061 --> 00:24:07,520
The Mackinac Bridge has a capacity of 6,000 cars per hour.

312
00:24:08,620 --> 00:24:11,080
The Mackinac Bridge will return on Modern Marvels.

313
00:24:11,860 --> 00:24:15,600
We now return to the Mackinac Bridge on Modern Marvels.

314
00:24:18,500 --> 00:24:24,880
In 1955, construction workers on the Mackinac Straits were busy building the foundation for the bridge

315
00:24:24,881 --> 00:24:27,400
that would finally link Michigan's two peninsulas.

316
00:24:27,401 --> 00:24:34,180
while the Mackinac bridges mammoth anchorages and tower supports were being built

317
00:24:34,181 --> 00:24:38,040
30 additional concrete piers were under construction as well

318
00:24:38,041 --> 00:24:43,040
built to support the roadway on either side of the suspension span

319
00:24:43,041 --> 00:24:47,940
crews erected the simple arch and t-shaped structures out of concrete and steel

320
00:24:47,941 --> 00:24:54,580
though dwarfed by the mammoth towers the additional supports were impressive in their own right

321
00:24:54,581 --> 00:24:57,800
There are 34 piers in the design of the Mackinac Bridge.

322
00:24:58,300 --> 00:25:03,720
Some of the spans between those individual piers equals the distance of some of the largest bridges in the country.

323
00:25:04,220 --> 00:25:09,080
Some of those lengths are about 500 to 600 feet long between some of the individual piers.

324
00:25:12,700 --> 00:25:20,340
While construction proceeded on the water, workers on the shore began building the three miles of trestle roadway that would make up the rest of the bridge.

325
00:25:20,341 --> 00:25:27,540
Some of the largest trestle pieces measured 472 feet in length and weighed 720 tons.

326
00:25:29,120 --> 00:25:36,040
Upon completion, tugboats towed the pieces on raised barges to their proper concrete anchorages, where they were lowered into place.

327
00:25:37,680 --> 00:25:41,420
Throughout the bridge-building process, engineers gambled with the changing weather.

328
00:25:42,660 --> 00:25:47,920
During construction, obviously one of the greatest difficulties is weather in the short construction season.

329
00:25:47,921 --> 00:25:50,140
They had a deadline. They needed revenue.

330
00:25:50,340 --> 00:25:53,640
and they had a very short period of time to put it together.

331
00:25:53,740 --> 00:25:55,700
That means they pushed the construction seasons.

332
00:25:55,880 --> 00:25:57,080
Weather was always a factor.

333
00:25:59,640 --> 00:26:01,900
On November 10, 1955,

334
00:26:02,420 --> 00:26:05,400
one of the region's infamous storms hammered the Straits.

335
00:26:06,140 --> 00:26:08,480
With winds gusting nearly 80 miles an hour,

336
00:26:09,000 --> 00:26:11,640
the blizzard nearly toppled one of the elevated trestles.

337
00:26:13,080 --> 00:26:15,620
Nine days later, the weather was finally good enough

338
00:26:15,621 --> 00:26:18,260
for the tugs to move the undamaged trestle into place.

339
00:26:18,261 --> 00:26:26,760
The day after the span was installed, the freezing winter weather returned, and the engineers were taking no more chances.

340
00:26:27,640 --> 00:26:30,300
Construction on the water was halted for the rest of the season.

341
00:26:33,980 --> 00:26:41,420
Though all work ceased on the water, throughout the winter and into the spring of 1956, iron workers remained busy.

342
00:26:42,480 --> 00:26:45,340
Rail cars regularly brought thousands of tons of steel.

343
00:26:45,341 --> 00:26:51,420
workers prefabricated portions of the bridge on the shoreline of the frozen straits

344
00:26:51,421 --> 00:26:56,320
you see the footage of them working out there in their heavy equipment and garb in the wintertime

345
00:26:56,321 --> 00:27:00,660
breathing you know it must have been freezing by the way you see their breath amazing group

346
00:27:00,661 --> 00:27:09,060
of men who put that bridge together in the spring of 1956 workers were back on the lake to begin

347
00:27:09,061 --> 00:27:14,860
construction on the most daring part of the job creating the main cables whenever you build a

348
00:27:14,861 --> 00:27:18,100
suspension bridge there are always massive cables involved in the

349
00:27:18,101 --> 00:27:22,180
construction you have two cables that hold up the roadway and those cables are

350
00:27:22,181 --> 00:27:25,780
made up of thousands of small cables small wires that have to be spun back

351
00:27:25,781 --> 00:27:28,480
and forth between the two towers the reason they do that is because it'd be

352
00:27:28,481 --> 00:27:36,100
impossible to run a cable that size at distance at one time to start the

353
00:27:36,101 --> 00:27:40,340
spinning process iron workers strung guide wires from the anchorages over

354
00:27:40,341 --> 00:27:41,402
both towers.

355
00:27:41,403 --> 00:27:48,660
Onto these wires they hung a safety catwalk made from timber and chain link fence.

356
00:27:51,280 --> 00:27:56,000
Ironically, what was meant to protect and keep the workers safe high above the wind churned water

357
00:27:56,001 --> 00:28:03,951
would instead bring terror and death. 756, they were kicking out the catwalk.

358
00:28:03,952 --> 00:28:06,120
I wasn't right there.

359
00:28:06,700 --> 00:28:08,920
I was on Pier 17 when it happened.

360
00:28:09,080 --> 00:28:10,940
That was about a mile from there.

361
00:28:12,840 --> 00:28:15,140
Over 500 feet above the water,

362
00:28:15,740 --> 00:28:19,680
a crew of workers was paying out the walkway when things went terribly wrong.

363
00:28:20,400 --> 00:28:23,060
The catwalk broke free from its guide wires.

364
00:28:24,120 --> 00:28:27,100
As their safety net disappeared from beneath their feet,

365
00:28:27,780 --> 00:28:32,040
two men, Jack C. Baker and Robert Copin, plummeted to their deaths.

366
00:28:32,041 --> 00:28:37,040
Another man was left dangling above the water, clinging to the collapsed catwalk,

367
00:28:37,041 --> 00:28:39,040
as stunned co-workers tried to rescue him.

368
00:28:39,041 --> 00:28:44,040
One guy on the tower that didn't fall, I met him and he said it was like a movie.

369
00:28:44,041 --> 00:28:49,040
Koppen and Baker were just tumbling in the air.

370
00:28:49,041 --> 00:28:53,040
And then one other guy went down with him, Stettman.

371
00:28:53,041 --> 00:28:59,040
But what happened there, the snow fence hit him as he went down

372
00:28:59,041 --> 00:29:07,040
down and he got a hold of the snow fence and he kicked his shoes off and climbed up to the top

373
00:29:07,041 --> 00:29:16,320
and got back on the tower and that was quite a climb remarkably with all the inherent dangers

374
00:29:16,321 --> 00:29:23,920
involved in bridge construction the death toll was unexpectedly low five people did lose their lives

375
00:29:23,921 --> 00:29:28,420
One of the divers who was working welding on the foundations, his name is Frank Pepper,

376
00:29:28,780 --> 00:29:32,040
came up too fast and died shortly afterwards of Ben's.

377
00:29:34,080 --> 00:29:38,980
The second man, James R. Lesarge, lost his balance working on one of the anchorages.

378
00:29:40,040 --> 00:29:43,780
While a third, Albert Abbott, apparently suffered a heart attack,

379
00:29:44,100 --> 00:29:46,440
walking a beam only a few feet above the water.

380
00:29:46,441 --> 00:29:54,480
The fall of Baker and Copen in June of 1956 marked the last fatalities the construction would see

381
00:29:54,481 --> 00:30:02,500
With the safety catwalk now squarely in place

382
00:30:02,501 --> 00:30:05,900
Bridge men began to spin the two main suspension cables

383
00:30:05,901 --> 00:30:09,260
The operation was a race against time and nature

384
00:30:09,261 --> 00:30:17,180
It wasn't a new process, but it had never been used before on such a large span.

385
00:30:18,120 --> 00:30:22,460
The strength of the unprotected wires could be compromised and left exposed to the elements.

386
00:30:25,260 --> 00:30:29,580
Two spinning wheels, complete with cowbells to alert the cable-laying bridge workers,

387
00:30:30,060 --> 00:30:32,740
were pulled from anchorage to anchorage over the two towers.

388
00:30:33,880 --> 00:30:37,220
The speed of the operation meant the workers had to stay on their toes.

389
00:30:37,221 --> 00:30:44,000
Just this side of Tower 20, when we were spinning the wire, that wheel ran 35 miles an hour.

390
00:30:44,180 --> 00:30:45,320
The wind was blowing pretty good.

391
00:30:46,480 --> 00:30:51,360
So I grabbed the two dead wires and put them down in the wickets, which was just some hooks.

392
00:30:52,060 --> 00:30:57,660
I reached up to grab a live wire, and when I did, two ferrules come together in that wind.

393
00:30:58,220 --> 00:31:00,900
And they snarled up, and she pinched me right there like that.

394
00:31:01,780 --> 00:31:03,960
35 miles an hour, I went down that walkway.

395
00:31:03,961 --> 00:31:07,420
And Ori Taylor shut the panic button off and stopped the wheel.

396
00:31:08,080 --> 00:31:10,620
And when I came back, I landed on the walkway.

397
00:31:11,460 --> 00:31:16,380
But I tore about 50% of my clothes off and tore hip up a little bit.

398
00:31:16,660 --> 00:31:19,820
But I landed on the walkway instead of in the drink.

399
00:31:20,140 --> 00:31:23,460
So that was a good experience.

400
00:31:26,600 --> 00:31:32,560
With each 3.25 mile round trip, the wheels deposited eight wires and took about a half hour.

401
00:31:33,520 --> 00:31:37,560
Workers bound 340 individual wires to make one strand.

402
00:31:38,440 --> 00:31:41,220
37 strands were combined to make one cable.

403
00:31:42,100 --> 00:31:46,980
Here's an actual piece of the wire that they used to string to make up the two main cables of the Mackinac Bridge.

404
00:31:47,080 --> 00:31:51,540
They used over 12,350 strands of wire to make up the two main cables.

405
00:31:52,120 --> 00:31:55,560
As you can see, it's pretty strong stuff. It's even hard to bend.

406
00:31:58,120 --> 00:32:00,820
The spinning continued 24 hours a day.

407
00:32:00,821 --> 00:32:11,040
300 men, 150 per side, worked 12-hour shifts, all the while engaged in a friendly competition to see who could wire the bridge fastest.

408
00:32:11,041 --> 00:32:16,700
When it was all done, there wasn't a dozen trips difference between the two shifts.

409
00:32:17,520 --> 00:32:21,720
And with all the shutdowns and everything else, it came out almost even.

410
00:32:22,140 --> 00:32:25,180
Each shift had done the same amount of trips across it.

411
00:32:25,181 --> 00:32:33,680
Over the course of 78 days, the workers strung and bound 41,000 miles of steel wire to build

412
00:32:33,681 --> 00:32:38,580
the two 24 and a half inch diameter main bridge cables.

413
00:32:38,581 --> 00:32:47,400
Their next big challenge, hanging the massive steel framework to form the roadway.

414
00:32:47,401 --> 00:32:51,620
Almost five million rivets were used in the construction of the Mackinac Bridge.

415
00:32:51,621 --> 00:32:55,460
The Mackinac Bridge will return on Modern Marvels.

416
00:32:58,140 --> 00:33:01,860
We now return to the Mackinac Bridge on Modern Marvels.

417
00:33:04,360 --> 00:33:09,660
During 1956, the high-profile high-wire act of the cable spinners

418
00:33:09,661 --> 00:33:12,260
mesmerized workers and spectators alike.

419
00:33:13,280 --> 00:33:16,940
While closer to the water, another less visible group was hard at work

420
00:33:16,941 --> 00:33:20,920
on the three miles of trestled roadway connecting the suspension bridge to land.

421
00:33:21,620 --> 00:33:28,940
By December of 1956, all but the two pieces of truss closest to the anchorages had been

422
00:33:28,941 --> 00:33:30,780
installed.

423
00:33:30,781 --> 00:33:35,740
The engineers pushed the season, hoping to finish, but the weather did not cooperate.

424
00:33:35,741 --> 00:33:41,620
Bone-chilling wind brought winter to the straits, and once again all work on the water was forced

425
00:33:41,621 --> 00:33:46,060
to stop, leaving the bridge with a strange gap-tooth appearance.

426
00:33:46,061 --> 00:33:51,620
The following spring, workers and engineers returned to the bridge with an uneasy sense

427
00:33:51,621 --> 00:33:52,620
of urgency.

428
00:33:52,621 --> 00:33:58,440
The completion date was slightly more than six months away, and there was still much

429
00:33:58,441 --> 00:33:59,440
to be done.

430
00:33:59,441 --> 00:34:04,340
For engineers, one of the most daunting jobs on the agenda was hanging the roadway onto

431
00:34:04,341 --> 00:34:06,980
the over 12,000-ton main cables.

432
00:34:06,981 --> 00:34:16,300
During the spring and summer of 1957, crane operators lifted 89 120 by 40 foot steel truss

433
00:34:16,301 --> 00:34:20,200
structures from barges anchored below the bridge.

434
00:34:20,201 --> 00:34:26,700
Iron workers standing 150 feet above the straights bolted the steel together and attached it

435
00:34:26,701 --> 00:34:31,460
to the 368 dangling wire suspender cables.

436
00:34:31,461 --> 00:34:36,800
If you can imagine the two towers with a cable between them, which is called a catenary,

437
00:34:36,801 --> 00:34:39,002
somewhat like a clothesline.

438
00:34:39,003 --> 00:34:43,800
If you were to put a piece of clothes, a heavy pair of jeans,

439
00:34:43,801 --> 00:34:47,442
on one end of your clothesline, the other end would go up.

440
00:34:47,443 --> 00:34:49,200
And that's the problems they

441
00:34:49,201 --> 00:34:55,800
had in construction of the roadway truss. In one of industry's first large-scale use

442
00:34:55,801 --> 00:35:00,800
of computers, engineers were able to calculate the exact order in which the pieces came into

443
00:35:00,801 --> 00:35:07,940
alignment. Every piece was mathematically considered so that the weight put on panel

444
00:35:07,941 --> 00:35:14,260
point two, when it was there, that means that panel point 46 prime was ready to be installed

445
00:35:14,261 --> 00:35:17,072
and they had to go through a certain sequence.

446
00:35:17,073 --> 00:35:19,700
They couldn't load one end up, put all your

447
00:35:19,701 --> 00:35:23,275
clothes on one end and the other end is just tight. They couldn't do that.

448
00:35:23,276 --> 00:35:24,000
So the Mackinac

449
00:35:24,001 --> 00:35:29,594
Bridge actually did not look in its graceful curve during the construction.

450
00:35:29,595 --> 00:35:30,340
It did not

451
00:35:30,341 --> 00:35:34,283
look that way into the installation of the last three roadway sections.

452
00:35:34,284 --> 00:35:35,060
The last three

