00:35:35,061 --> 00:35:38,160
truss sections were the center section and the one at each tower.

454
00:35:41,060 --> 00:35:45,360
By July 22nd, all but the last three pieces were in place.

455
00:35:47,940 --> 00:35:52,380
Green operators hoisted the building-sized trusses up to eager gangs of iron workers.

456
00:35:54,980 --> 00:35:59,820
With the roadway truss finished, the main cables settled into their final resting position.

457
00:36:00,340 --> 00:36:06,820
Work crews coated the bundle wires in red lead paste and then using a motorized wrapping

458
00:36:06,821 --> 00:36:11,100
machine wound them in a final galvanized steel sheath.

459
00:36:11,101 --> 00:36:17,740
The last step in completing the bridge was laying the roadway.

460
00:36:17,741 --> 00:36:23,100
First workers maneuvered and then secured giant steel stringers to the top of the truss.

461
00:36:23,101 --> 00:36:28,480
For aerodynamics and to lessen the weight, engineers installed a steel grate surface

462
00:36:28,481 --> 00:36:30,009
atop the stringers.

463
00:36:30,010 --> 00:36:35,320
During October of 1957, bridge workers made over one million welds

464
00:36:35,321 --> 00:36:36,722
to secure the grate.

465
00:36:36,723 --> 00:36:41,280
As the steel deck was being placed, a small army of pavers spread

466
00:36:41,281 --> 00:36:48,340
6,660 tons of concrete to form the four-lane road surface, including the two outer lanes

467
00:36:48,341 --> 00:36:49,470
on the suspension span.

468
00:36:49,471 --> 00:36:52,760
And they used lightweight concrete instead of the regular concrete,

469
00:36:53,140 --> 00:36:57,720
and also lightweight asphalt because it was a bridge and they wanted to make sure it was

470
00:36:57,721 --> 00:37:06,400
aerodynamic. Finally, after four years on November 1st, 1957, at a cost of a

471
00:37:06,401 --> 00:37:11,780
hundred million dollars, the bridge opened to traffic.

472
00:37:13,000 --> 00:37:17,480
Prentice M. Brown and Michigan's governor, G. Menon Williams, were among

473
00:37:17,481 --> 00:37:22,220
dignitaries in the first car to cross. But like everybody else, they had to pay

474
00:37:22,221 --> 00:37:24,500
the $3.25 toll.

475
00:37:26,620 --> 00:37:28,720
Fanfare and fireworks marked the event.

476
00:37:29,740 --> 00:37:32,080
But fearing the unpredictable November weather,

477
00:37:32,960 --> 00:37:35,860
the Bridge Authority scheduled the official opening festivities

478
00:37:35,861 --> 00:37:39,240
to be held in the warmth and calm of the following June.

479
00:37:40,520 --> 00:37:42,500
On that sunny November day,

480
00:37:42,880 --> 00:37:46,060
the people of Michigan realized they had to wait another six months

481
00:37:46,061 --> 00:37:47,560
for the real celebration to begin.

482
00:37:48,600 --> 00:37:52,100
But when that day came, the weather had one more surprise.

483
00:37:52,220 --> 00:37:59,100
It was a miserable, miserable day. The weather was so bad. Floats were destroyed.

484
00:37:59,101 --> 00:38:05,480
But you know it didn't dampen the spirits of the participants. They just

485
00:38:05,481 --> 00:38:10,340
waited until the weather cleared. They marched, they smiled, the bands played.

486
00:38:10,341 --> 00:38:15,900
It was a great wonderful day and it went on for the next six days.

487
00:38:15,901 --> 00:38:25,360
For over four years, bridge workers and engineers had endured everything that nature could throw

488
00:38:25,361 --> 00:38:28,420
at them.

489
00:38:28,421 --> 00:38:33,900
In the end, it seemed somehow fitting that bad weather returned for one last appearance.

490
00:38:33,901 --> 00:38:39,900
But on time and on budget, the builders had created something that no amount of wind or

491
00:38:39,901 --> 00:38:41,840
current could destroy.

492
00:38:42,740 --> 00:38:46,940
Now it fell to future bridge workers to make sure it stayed that way.

493
00:38:50,440 --> 00:38:55,540
The wire rope, which originally supported the safety catwalk, was recycled, cut into lengths,

494
00:38:55,640 --> 00:39:00,540
and fashioned into the suspender cables that today connect the roadway to the main cables.

495
00:39:01,640 --> 00:39:04,440
The Mackinac Bridge will return on Modern Marvels.

496
00:39:05,640 --> 00:39:08,940
We now return to the Mackinac Bridge on Modern Marvels.

497
00:39:11,840 --> 00:39:16,840
The engineers who built the Mighty Mac boasted that it would last a thousand years.

498
00:39:20,840 --> 00:39:25,840
That's possible, according to the Mackinac Bridge Authority, whose job it is to keep the bridge up and running.

499
00:39:29,840 --> 00:39:35,840
Sixty full-time workers are charged with everything from minor painting to changing the lights on the towers.

500
00:39:35,841 --> 00:39:40,657
hours. During the winter, almost all maintenance work is halted.

501
00:39:40,658 --> 00:39:42,840
But with each spring, an army

502
